<Ui>
    <!--
        The real client's UIErrorsFrame, now declared as what it actually IS: a
        `<MessageFrame>` (ref UIErrorsFrame.xml:4 — 512×60 at TOP (0,-122), displayDuration="5",
        insertMode="TOP", with `<FontString inherits="ErrorFont" justifyH="CENTER"/>` as its line
        font). UI_INFO_MESSAGE shows YELLOW (1,1,0) — the quest objective-progress toasts land
        here — and UI_ERROR_MESSAGE red (1,0.1,0.1), the ref handler verbatim (UIErrorsFrame.lua:8-16;
        its SYSMSG arm is unwired, no engine producer yet).

        It used to be a hand-rolled `<Frame>` painting three stacked FontStrings from Lua, because
        this engine had no MessageFrame widget. It has one now (`script::messageframe::plain`,
        `widget::MessageFrameState`), so the file that stood in for it is gone and the display —
        insertMode, the vertical cap, the two-phase fade — is the widget's own, exactly as in the
        reference. Two consequences worth naming:

          * `UIErrorsFrame:AddMessage(text, r, g, b, a)` is REAL now, so FrameXML producers call it
            the way the reference does (the old `BenillaErrorsFrame_AddMessage` seam is retired),
            and so does an addon: `EasyCopy`, `QuestHistory` and `QuestItem` each died on that exact
            line at load. A sixth argument (all three pass one, believing it is a hold time) is
            silently IGNORED — the real binding reads five, and honouring it would be unfaithful.
          * The post-hold fade is the class ctor's 3.0 s (msgframe-runtime.md, byte-verified), not
            the 1.0 s this file used to carry as an explicitly INTERIM guess while that curve was
            unpinned. The 5 s hold is unchanged.

        frameStrata HIGH is the ref's own (`UIErrorsFrame.xml:4`) and decision 0597's: this frame
        overlaps the right edge of every left-slot panel window (character, quest log, loot,
        merchant, mail, trainer, taxi — all at TOPLEFT (0,-104)), so in the default MEDIUM its
        toasts draw UNDER whichever panel is open, invisible in exactly the state you most want to
        read them. The ref also carries toplevel="true"; that is deliberately not transcribed —
        HIGH already wins the strata and 0597's fix is the one that was looked at.
    -->
    <Script><![CDATA[
        function UIErrorsFrame_OnLoad()
            this:RegisterEvent("UI_INFO_MESSAGE")
            this:RegisterEvent("UI_ERROR_MESSAGE")
        end

        -- ref UIErrorsFrame_OnEvent(event, message) (UIErrorsFrame.lua:8-15) — the message is the
        -- DECLARED second parameter, not the `arg1` global: the XML passes `arg1` into it exactly
        -- as the reference's own does, and an addon calling the 1.12 arity is then read correctly.
        function UIErrorsFrame_OnEvent(event, message)
            if not message then return end
            if event == "UI_INFO_MESSAGE" then
                this:AddMessage(message, 1.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0)  -- ref UIErrorsFrame.lua:12
            elseif event == "UI_ERROR_MESSAGE" then
                this:AddMessage(message, 1.0, 0.1, 0.1, 1.0)  -- ref UIErrorsFrame.lua:14
            end
        end
    ]]></Script>

    <MessageFrame name="UIErrorsFrame" toplevel="true" parent="UIParent" displayDuration="5" insertMode="TOP" frameStrata="HIGH">
        <Size><AbsDimension x="512" y="60"/></Size>
        <Anchors>
            <Anchor point="TOP" relativePoint="TOP">
                <Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-122"/></Offset>
            </Anchor>
        </Anchors>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>UIErrorsFrame_OnLoad()</OnLoad>
            <OnEvent>UIErrorsFrame_OnEvent(event, arg1)</OnEvent>
        </Scripts>
        <!-- The line font AND the centring, both: the class draws its messages through this
             declared child, so `justifyH="CENTER"` is what keeps the toasts centred in the 512-wide
             box (a chat frame's own child says LEFT). ErrorFont = GameFontNormalLarge (ref
             Fonts.xml:258), which is the face the hand-rolled version used too. -->
        <FontString inherits="ErrorFont" justifyH="CENTER"/>
    </MessageFrame>

    <!-- RaidWarningFrame (ref RaidWarning.xml:4-24), declared here beside the frame it anchors to.

         Two corpus addons push into it directly — `Participant/RaidWarn.lua:133` and
         `oRA2/RaidWarn.lua:133` are the same code in two distributions, plus their MainTank.lua's
         "Tank %s has died!" — and both call
         `RaidWarningFrame:AddMessage(text, r, g, b, 1.0, UIERRORS_HOLD_TIME)`. That is the plain
         MessageFrame arity we already ship: the 6th argument is read and DROPPED, because the
         reference's own `0x795590` reads five and stops (messageframe/plain.rs's first law).
         Absent, that line is `attempt to index global` and takes the handler with it.
         (`BigWigs` also names it — in a LOCALISATION STRING, "Suppressing RaidWarningFrame". Not a
         use; 1207.)

         Geometry quoted: 512x40, insertMode TOP, HIGH + toplevel, TOP anchored to UIErrorsFrame's
         BOTTOM at (0,-10), and the direct-child FontString the class draws its lines through —
         GameFontNormalHuge, CENTER, exactly as the sibling above takes ErrorFont.

         **No <Scripts>, deliberately.** The reference's OnLoad/OnEvent call into RaidWarning.lua to
         register CHAT_MSG_RAID_WARNING and route it here. We already deliver that chat type through
         `ui_chat::feed`, so wiring a second consumer would print every raid warning TWICE; and
         declaring handlers whose functions we do not ship would raise at load. The frame exists and
         accepts messages, which is what the corpus asks of it — the automatic routing is the
         ChatFrame_OnEvent architecture question, not this frame's.

         RaidBossEmoteFrame, its sibling in the same file, is NOT declared: zero corpus addons name
         it, and a shadowed name with no caller is building against a guess (1222's rejected clause). -->
    <MessageFrame name="RaidWarningFrame" parent="UIParent" insertMode="TOP" frameStrata="HIGH" toplevel="true">
        <Size><AbsDimension x="512" y="40"/></Size>
        <Anchors>
            <Anchor point="TOP" relativeTo="UIErrorsFrame" relativePoint="BOTTOM">
                <Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-10"/></Offset>
            </Anchor>
        </Anchors>
        <FontString inherits="GameFontNormalHuge" justifyH="CENTER"/>
    </MessageFrame>
</Ui>
